/// A Wi-Fi radio, **seen from the station manager** (docs/wifi-prerequisites-plan.md
/// section 3.5, docs/wifi-plan.md section 5.1). The chip driver (`wifid`, uid
/// `_wifi`, holds `CAP_DEV_CLAIM` and the bus) serves it; its one client is
/// `wlanmd` (uid `_wlan`, no capabilities). The simulator `wifisim` (`_wifisim`)
/// serves the same interface in CI and is its first implementation, which is
/// why nothing here is shaped by one chip.
///
/// **Trust.** The side that holds DMA parses nothing from the air and the side
/// that parses the air holds no capabilities. So this interface moves
/// *opaque management frames* in both directions: the driver hands over raw
/// beacon, probe-response, authentication and association bodies exactly as
/// the radio received them (with the radio's own metadata: channel, signal,
/// age) and transmits frames `wlanmd` built, and it never looks inside an
/// information element. Everything the driver does decide is a hardware
/// matter: which channels it may tune (it enforces the country it was
/// given), key slots, and the association state its firmware needs.
///
/// **The data path is not here.** Once the station is authorized, payload
/// frames travel as 802.3 Ethernet over `os.lazy.net.nic.v1`, which the same
/// driver serves to `netd` (the chip does the 802.3 to 802.11 conversion and
/// the encryption). The NIC's link state follows this interface: it is down
/// until `SetState(Authorized)`. A driver whose chip cannot convert frames is
/// out of scope for v1.
///
/// **One client.** The first caller of `Attach` owns the radio; its identity
/// is the kernel-stamped sender, and every later call or `Detach` by anyone
/// else fails with `EACCES` (a second attach with `EBUSY`). The owner's exit,
/// or its event channel reporting the peer gone, detaches it and leaves the
/// radio idle: scanning stops, the BSS is left, all keys are deleted.
///
/// **Events.** `Attach` carries the client's event channel; the driver sends
/// the `oneway` methods below (`ScanDone`, `RxMgmt`, `BeaconLoss`,
/// `Deauthenticated`) on it. They carry the same bounds as calls: a frame is
/// at most `HwInfo.max_mgmt` bytes, never truncated and never delivered
/// partially; one that does not fit is dropped and counted.
///
/// **Keys cross this interface.** `SetKey` is the one place secret material
/// (a temporal key) leaves `wlanmd`: the hardware needs it to encrypt. It is
/// accepted only from the owner, the driver never logs or stores it beyond
/// the key slot, and it is cleared by `DelKey`, `Leave` and `Detach`.
///
/// Failures of calls are returned as the shared structured error field
/// (`services::error_field`) instead of the declared reply fields.
interface os.lazy.net.wifi.hw.v1 {
    /// Describe the radio: addresses, bands, ciphers, limits. Any caller the
    /// label policy lets reach the driver may ask, attached or not: it reveals
    /// only hardware facts.
    method Info() -> (info: HwInfo = 1);
    /// Become the radio's one client. `events` is the channel the driver
    /// sends events on. `EBUSY` when attached.
    method Attach(events: Channel<os.lazy.net.wifi.hw.v1> = 1) -> ();
    /// Release the radio (also implied when the owner exits). Leaves the BSS,
    /// aborts a scan and deletes every key.
    method Detach() -> ();
    /// Tell the radio which regulatory domain applies: an ISO 3166 alpha-2
    /// code, or `00` for the world domain (passive scanning only on channels
    /// where transmitting first is not universally allowed, no 6 GHz). The
    /// driver must not transmit on a channel the domain forbids and may refuse
    /// to transmit at all before the first call (`EAGAIN` from `Scan` with
    /// active probing, `Join`, `TxMgmt`). A code it does not know is `EINVAL`.
    /// The value comes from `wlanmd`, which reads `sys/net/wifi/country`;
    /// the driver trusts nobody else for it.
    method SetCountry(alpha2: String = 1) -> ();
    /// Start a scan. The reply is the id `ScanDone` and `ScanResults` name; it
    /// does not wait. Only one scan runs at a time (`EBUSY`). At most
    /// `HwInfo.max_scan_ssids` SSIDs of at most 32 bytes and at most
    /// `HwInfo.max_scan_channels` channels (`EINVAL` beyond, or for a channel
    /// the radio or its domain does not have). A scan during an association
    /// is allowed and may pause traffic for its dwell time.
    method Scan(request: ScanRequest = 1) -> (scan_id: U32 = 1);
    /// Stop the running scan early; `ScanDone` follows with `aborted` set.
    /// Not an error when none runs.
    method AbortScan(scan_id: U32 = 1) -> ();
    /// One page of a finished (or running) scan's results, in the order the
    /// radio heard them, from entry `first`; at most `max` entries (capped at
    /// 64). `more` says whether a later page holds entries. Each entry is the
    /// raw management frame body, **unparsed**: parsing it is `wlanmd`'s job.
    /// An unknown `scan_id` is `ENOENT`.
    method ScanResults(scan_id: U32 = 1, first: U32 = 2, max: U32 = 3) -> (results: Array<ScanEntry> = 1, more: Bool = 2);
    /// Tune to the BSS's channel and filter to its address so `TxMgmt` and
    /// `RxMgmt` carry the authentication and association exchange. Leaves any
    /// previous BSS first. The reply means the radio is on the channel, not
    /// that anything was heard.
    method Join(request: JoinRequest = 1) -> ();
    /// Send a management or EAPOL frame `wlanmd` built, as is. `kind` is a
    /// `FrameKind` ordinal; for `Mgmt` the frame is a complete 802.11
    /// management frame without FCS, for `Eapol` a complete 802.3 EAPOL frame
    /// (the radio adds the 802.11 header and, once keys are installed, the
    /// encryption). Only while joined (`ENOTCONN`); a frame longer than
    /// `HwInfo.max_mgmt` is `EINVAL`. The reply means queued, not delivered.
    method TxMgmt(kind: U32 = 1, frame: Bytes = 2) -> ();
    /// Move the radio's association state: a `StaState` ordinal. `Associated`
    /// carries the association id the AP assigned; `Authorized` opens the
    /// controlled port (data frames flow, the NIC's link comes up) and is
    /// valid only after the pairwise key is installed. Going to a lower state
    /// is always allowed and takes the link down.
    method SetState(state: U32 = 1, aid: U32 = 2) -> ();
    /// Install a key in a hardware slot. `kind` is a `KeyKind` ordinal,
    /// `index` the key id (pairwise 0, group 1 to 3, IGTK 4 to 5), `cipher`
    /// a `Cipher` ordinal the radio advertised, `key` its exact length,
    /// `rsc` the receive sequence counter the handshake delivered (little
    /// endian, up to 8 bytes) and `addr` the peer address (the BSSID for a
    /// group key). Replaces a key in the same slot atomically. `EINVAL` for a
    /// length or index that does not fit, `ENOSPC` for no free slot.
    method SetKey(kind: U32 = 1, index: U32 = 2, cipher: U32 = 3, key: Bytes = 4, rsc: Bytes = 5, addr: Bytes = 6) -> ();
    /// Remove a key; not an error when the slot is empty.
    method DelKey(kind: U32 = 1, index: U32 = 2) -> ();
    /// Leave the BSS: a `Deauthentication` with this IEEE 802.11 reason code
    /// is sent if the radio can, every key is deleted, the NIC's link goes
    /// down and the radio returns to idle. Not an error when not joined.
    method Leave(reason: U32 = 1) -> ();
    /// Counters since the driver started.
    method Stats() -> (stats: HwStats = 1);

    /// Event, driver to client: the scan finished (`aborted` false) or was
    /// stopped (`aborted` true, or the radio could not finish); its results
    /// are readable with `ScanResults` until the next `Scan`.
    method ScanDone(scan_id: U32 = 1, aborted: Bool = 2) -> () oneway;
    /// Event, driver to client: a management or EAPOL frame addressed to this
    /// station arrived from the joined BSS. `kind` is a `FrameKind` ordinal,
    /// `rssi_dbm` the signal in dBm. The bytes are unparsed and hostile.
    method RxMgmt(kind: U32 = 1, rssi_dbm: I32 = 2, frame: Bytes = 3) -> () oneway;
    /// Event, driver to client: the radio stopped hearing the AP (the
    /// firmware's missed-beacon threshold). The client decides what to do.
    method BeaconLoss() -> () oneway;
    /// Event, driver to client: the AP deauthenticated or disassociated us.
    /// `reason` is the IEEE 802.11 reason code, taken from the frame by the
    /// firmware (the one field of a frame the driver reports; the frame
    /// itself is also delivered as `RxMgmt`).
    method Deauthenticated(reason: U32 = 1) -> () oneway;

    /// What the radio is.
    struct HwInfo {
        /// Six octets, network order: the station address (also the NIC's).
        mac: Bytes = 1,
        /// The registry name of the radio's `os.lazy.net.nic.v1` interface
        /// (`wlan0`): how `wlanmd` names the interface in topics.
        interface: String = 2,
        /// Bands, a `Band` bitmap: bit *n* for the `Band` with ordinal *n*.
        bands: U32 = 3,
        /// Ciphers the hardware can run, a `Cipher` bitmap.
        ciphers: U32 = 4,
        /// Whether the firmware scans on its own (offload) rather than being
        /// stepped by the host.
        scan_offload: Bool = 5,
        /// Largest management frame in either direction, in bytes.
        max_mgmt: U32 = 6,
        /// Most SSIDs one scan may probe for.
        max_scan_ssids: U32 = 7,
        /// Most channels one scan may name.
        max_scan_channels: U32 = 8,
        /// Hardware key slots (pairwise and group together).
        key_slots: U32 = 9,
        /// Feature bits, zero if none. Reserved bits are zero; a client
        /// ignores them.
        features: U32 = 10
    }

    /// What to scan. Channel numbers are 802.11 channel numbers within a
    /// band, with `band` selecting which.
    struct ScanRequest {
        /// Channels to visit; empty means every channel the radio and the
        /// regulatory domain allow.
        channels: Array<U32> = 1,
        /// A `Band` ordinal, used when `channels` is empty to limit the sweep
        /// to one band; `U32::MAX` means all bands.
        band: U32 = 2,
        /// SSIDs to probe for (hidden networks); empty is a wildcard probe.
        ssids: Array<Bytes> = 3,
        /// Probe actively where the domain allows; otherwise listen only.
        active: Bool = 4,
        /// Milliseconds to stay on each channel, 0 for the driver's default.
        dwell_ms: U32 = 5
    }

    /// One heard frame: a beacon or probe response, unparsed.
    struct ScanEntry {
        /// The complete management frame body (without FCS), as received.
        frame: Bytes = 1,
        /// The channel it was heard on (a number within `band`).
        channel: U32 = 2,
        /// A `Band` ordinal.
        band: U32 = 3,
        /// Signal strength in dBm as the radio measured it.
        rssi_dbm: I32 = 4,
        /// Milliseconds before the results were read that it was heard.
        age_ms: U32 = 5
    }

    /// Where to go.
    struct JoinRequest {
        /// Six octets: the BSSID to authenticate with.
        bssid: Bytes = 1,
        channel: U32 = 2,
        /// A `Band` ordinal.
        band: U32 = 3,
        /// A `Width` ordinal: the widest channel the client wants.
        width: U32 = 4
    }

    /// Counters since the driver started.
    struct HwStats {
        scans: U64 = 1,
        mgmt_rx: U64 = 2,
        mgmt_tx: U64 = 3,
        /// Frames dropped because the client's queue was full or a frame was
        /// over `max_mgmt`.
        mgmt_dropped: U64 = 4,
        beacon_losses: U64 = 5,
        deauths: U64 = 6,
        /// Firmware errors and timeouts the driver recovered from.
        fw_errors: U64 = 7,
        /// Times the radio was reset.
        resets: U64 = 8
    }

    /// Frame classes for `TxMgmt` and `RxMgmt`. `Mgmt`: an 802.11 management
    /// frame (authentication, association, deauthentication, action). `Eapol`:
    /// an EAPOL frame as 802.3 (the handshake runs before the port opens).
    enum FrameKind { Mgmt, Eapol }

    /// Bands; ordinals are the `bands` bit numbers, so new ones are
    /// append-only.
    enum Band { Ghz2, Ghz5, Ghz6 }

    /// Channel widths.
    enum Width { Mhz20, Mhz40, Mhz80, Mhz160 }

    /// Ciphers; ordinals are the `ciphers` bit numbers. IEEE 802.11 suite
    /// selectors: `Ccmp128` is 00-0F-AC:4, `Gcmp256` 00-0F-AC:9, `BipCmac128`
    /// 00-0F-AC:6 (the IGTK cipher).
    enum Cipher { Ccmp128, Gcmp256, BipCmac128 }

    /// Key classes.
    enum KeyKind { Pairwise, Group, Igtk }

    /// The radio's association state, in order: each state implies the ones
    /// before it. `Idle`: not joined. `Authenticated` and `Associated` mirror
    /// the 802.11 state machine. `Authorized`: the 4-way handshake finished
    /// and the controlled port is open.
    enum StaState { Idle, Authenticated, Associated, Authorized }
}

/// The Wi-Fi station service, **the system-facing interface** (docs/wifi-prerequisites-plan.md
/// sections 3.5 and 3.7). `wlanmd` (uid `_wlan`, no capabilities) serves it
/// under the registry name `os.lazy.net.wifi` and runs the station state
/// machine over `os.lazy.net.wifi.hw.v1` and `keyd`. Front ends (the Network
/// app, the tray applet, `wifictl`, `rhai`) are its clients.
///
/// **Who may do what** (the caller is the kernel-stamped sender; `elevd` is a
/// recognised identity, never a claim in a request):
///
/// * Reading (`Networks`, `Status`) and `Scan`: any logged-in session user.
/// * `Connect`, `Disconnect`, `AddNetwork` with scope `User`, and `Forget` of
///   the caller's own network: any session user, for networks they own.
/// * `AddNetwork` with scope `System`, `Forget` of a system network: only the
///   `elevd` identity, after the administrator approved the prompt (action
///   `net.wifi.system`); a session user gets `EPERM`. `Connect` to a system
///   network is allowed to anyone (the secret never leaves `keyd`).
///
/// **Secrets.** A passphrase is accepted on exactly one call, `AddNetwork`;
/// `wlanmd` passes it to `keyd` at once, keeps nothing and never logs it, and
/// no method ever returns one. Everything else here is non-secret: SSIDs,
/// BSSIDs, security type, signal. A profile is stored in `confd` (system
/// networks under `sys/net/wifi/networks/<id>`, a user's under
/// `user/<uid>/net/wifi/networks/<id>`); the secret lives in `keyd` under the
/// same `<id>` and scope.
///
/// **Hostile input.** `Networks` and the scan topic show the result of parsing
/// beacons that any radio in range can send. Names are length-capped and
/// stripped of control characters by `wlanmd` before they get here, but a
/// client still treats an SSID as untrusted text (never as markup, never as a
/// path or command).
///
/// Every method takes the interface name (`wlan0`) the registry gave the
/// radio; a name `wlanmd` does not manage is `ENODEV`. Failures reply with the
/// shared structured error field (`services::error_field`).
interface os.lazy.net.wifi.v1 {
    /// The wireless interfaces `wlanmd` manages, by name (`wlan0`).
    method Interfaces() -> (names: Array<String> = 1);
    /// Ask for a fresh scan; returns at once, the results arrive on
    /// `system/net/{ifname}/wifi/scan`. `ssids` are hidden networks to probe
    /// for (at most 4); empty is a wildcard scan. A scan is refused while one
    /// is under way (`EBUSY`) and may be skipped while the link is busy with
    /// the handshake.
    method Scan(ifname: String = 1, ssids: Array<String> = 2) -> ();
    /// The current scan results merged with the known profiles: one entry per
    /// network (SSID and security), strongest BSS first, with the profile id
    /// when the caller may use a known network. Profiles of other users are
    /// not shown. At most 128 entries.
    method Networks(ifname: String = 1) -> (list: Array<Network> = 1);
    /// Remember a network and its secret. `profile.id` must be empty (the
    /// service picks one and returns it); `scope` is a `Scope` ordinal. The
    /// passphrase is required for `Wpa2Psk` (8 to 63 printable ASCII
    /// characters, or exactly 64 hex digits for a raw PSK) and must be absent
    /// for `Open`. Does not connect. `EEXIST` for the same SSID and scope;
    /// `EINVAL` for a bad SSID (1 to 32 bytes), security or passphrase;
    /// `EPERM` for scope `System` from anyone but `elevd`.
    method AddNetwork(profile: Profile = 1, passphrase: Option<String> = 2) -> (id: String = 1);
    /// Join the network `id` on `ifname`: leave the current one, scan if the
    /// SSID is not in the results, pick the best BSS, authenticate, associate
    /// and run the handshake. Returns when the attempt is started; the
    /// outcome is on `system/net/{ifname}/wifi/state` (`Connected` or
    /// `Failed` with a reason). A user network belongs to its owner: another
    /// user gets `EPERM`.
    method Connect(ifname: String = 1, id: String = 2) -> ();
    /// Leave the current network and do not reconnect until asked (auto-
    /// connect is suspended for this interface until the next `Connect`).
    /// Not an error when not connected.
    method Disconnect(ifname: String = 1) -> ();
    /// Delete a profile and its secret. Disconnects first if it is the
    /// current network. Not an error for an id that is gone.
    method Forget(id: String = 1) -> ();
    /// The interface's state now: the same value the retained `state` topic
    /// carries, for a client that does not subscribe.
    method Status(ifname: String = 1) -> (status: WifiState = 1);

    /// A heard network, merged with what is known about it.
    struct Network {
        /// UTF-8 text sanitised by the service, at most 32 bytes. Empty for a
        /// hidden network that is not a known profile (shown by BSSID only).
        ssid: String = 1,
        /// A `Security` ordinal.
        security: U32 = 2,
        /// Signal of the strongest BSS in dBm.
        rssi_dbm: I32 = 3,
        /// Frequency in MHz of that BSS.
        freq_mhz: U32 = 4,
        /// How many BSSes of this network were heard.
        bss_count: U32 = 5,
        /// The profile id when this is a known network the caller may use;
        /// empty otherwise.
        id: String = 6,
        /// Whether this is the network the interface is connected to.
        connected: Bool = 7
    }

    /// A network to remember. Non-secret fields only (the passphrase travels
    /// as `AddNetwork`'s own argument).
    struct Profile {
        /// Empty on `AddNetwork`; set when listed.
        id: String = 1,
        ssid: String = 2,
        /// A `Security` ordinal.
        security: U32 = 3,
        /// Probe for the SSID even when no beacon shows it.
        hidden: Bool = 4,
        /// Join it automatically when in range.
        autoconnect: Bool = 5,
        /// Higher wins when several known networks are in range.
        priority: U32 = 6,
        /// A `Scope` ordinal.
        scope: U32 = 7
    }

    /// An interface's connection state: the payload of
    /// `system/net/{ifname}/wifi/state`.
    struct WifiState {
        /// A `ConnState` ordinal.
        state: U32 = 1,
        /// The network joined or being joined (empty when none).
        ssid: String = 2,
        /// Six octets, the BSSID in use (empty when none).
        bssid: Bytes = 3,
        /// Signal of the current BSS in dBm (0 when not connected).
        rssi_dbm: I32 = 4,
        freq_mhz: U32 = 5,
        /// The profile id in use (empty when none).
        id: String = 6,
        /// A `FailReason` ordinal; meaningful when `state` is `Failed`,
        /// `None` otherwise.
        reason: U32 = 7,
        /// Changes since the service started, so a subscriber can tell a
        /// repeat from a flap.
        changes: U32 = 8
    }

    /// A scan finished: the payload of `system/net/{ifname}/wifi/scan`. It
    /// carries no networks (those come from `Networks`, which is filtered
    /// per caller); it only says the list changed.
    struct ScanEvent {
        /// Scans completed since the service started.
        generation: U32 = 1,
        /// Networks in the merged list.
        count: U32 = 2
    }

    /// The interface's state, retained so a late subscriber (the tray applet
    /// after login) sees it at once. `{ifname}` is the radio's interface name.
    topic "system/net/{ifname}/wifi/state" : WifiState retained qos=latest;

    /// A scan completed and `Networks` changed. Not retained: a client that
    /// arrives later calls `Networks`.
    topic "system/net/{ifname}/wifi/scan" : ScanEvent qos=latest;

    /// Security types. `Wpa2Psk` is WPA2-Personal with CCMP (AKM 2, or AKM 6
    /// when the AP offers PSK-SHA256 and management-frame protection).
    /// Ordinals are append-only: WPA3-SAE and enterprise are not offered yet,
    /// and a scan result of a type the service cannot join is reported as
    /// `Unsupported` rather than hidden.
    enum Security { Open, Wpa2Psk, Unsupported }

    /// Who owns a profile. `User`: the caller, available while they are logged
    /// in. `System`: everyone, including before login (needs `elevd`).
    enum Scope { User, System }

    /// The connection state machine. `Disabled`: no radio or the service is
    /// stopped. `Idle`: nothing to do. `Scanning`, `Authenticating`,
    /// `Associating`, `Handshake`: the steps of a join. `Connected`: the port
    /// is open and the NIC's link is up. `Failed`: the last attempt ended,
    /// see `FailReason`; the service retries with back-off unless the user
    /// disconnected.
    enum ConnState { Disabled, Idle, Scanning, Authenticating, Associating, Handshake, Connected, Failed }

    /// Why an attempt failed. `None`: no failure. `NotFound`: no BSS of the
    /// SSID was heard. `Rejected`: the AP refused authentication or
    /// association. `BadPassphrase`: the handshake's MIC did not verify
    /// (indistinguishable from an attack on the air, so the message is
    /// worded as a probable wrong passphrase). `Timeout`: an expected frame
    /// never came. `Deauthenticated`: the AP ended an established link.
    /// `BeaconLoss`: the AP went silent. `PolicyMismatch`: the AP's security
    /// settings differ between its beacon and the handshake. `Radio`: the
    /// driver reported an error.
    enum FailReason { None, NotFound, Rejected, BadPassphrase, Timeout, Deauthenticated, BeaconLoss, PolicyMismatch, Radio }
}
